## Case text (Ask-CK ck.db zephyr_cases, key AWPTCM-T46421)
Title: Link flap
Objective: (empty in ck.db)
Precondition: conduct link flap at supported speed and check status
Steps: (empty in ck.db -- no step text, no expected result). Method applied (triage 2026-10-21, agreed by the Test Engineer):
flap the one link both ways, at every speed the link partner supports: local flaps = shutdown / no shutdown on the DUT's port1.0.1;
remote flaps = shutdown / no shutdown on the AR4050S's port1.0.2; three flaps at each of auto 1000, auto 100 and auto 10 (the speed set
on the DUT with `speed auto <n>`); check = the port status returns to connected at the expected negotiated speed and duplex, the LLDP
neighbour is re-learned, and the DUT log shows one "Port down notification" + one "Port up notification" per flap (plus
"user shutdown"/"user no shutdown" for the local ones). Wider than the 2026-10-09 run (1000 only: the IE520 partner refused 10).

## Method / tools
tools/tb ckcon tb470 pp1323 <uN> 115200 <commands> with the `@poll <s> <regex> <cmd>` directive (re-reads `do show interface portX status`
from config-if mode until the regex matches; ~1.2 s per read, so a convergence time is poll-granular). Stdouts kept by the tester as
46421-u1a.out (health before + 9 local flaps), 46421-u0b1.out (3 remote flaps at 1000), 46421-u1b2.out (DUT to auto 100),
46421-u0b2.out (3 remote flaps at 100), 46421-u1b3.out (DUT to auto 10), 46421-u0b3.out (3 remote flaps at 10), 46421-u1c.out
(DUT restore to auto, final reads, health after, running-config), 46421-u0c.out (4050 running-config, boot, reboot history).
Every block below is verbatim from those files; the HH:MM:SS >>> stamps are the box's clock (NZDT); DUT log times are UTC (13 h behind).
Setup: none (the baseline link is the test link). .cfg files: swi_f.cfg from 46421-u1a.out's running-config (13:5x, before step 1),
swi_e.cfg from base-u0.out (13:48:26, before step 1). Teardown: `no speed` on the DUT (sent at 13:5x, below) and `no shutdown` already
in place on both ends; proven by tools/rcdiff.py (both units: no difference vs the pre-run capture) and the status/LLDP reads.

## RESULT SUMMARY (graded from the blocks below)
Local flaps on x230 port1.0.1 (step A, 13:52:10-13:53:27 box clock):
  at auto 1000: 3 flaps; shutdown -> "disabled" at the first read (1.2 s); no shutdown -> "connected trunk a-full a-1000" in 4.5 s (3 reads) each.
  speed auto 100 -> a-full a-100 in 4.5 s; 3 flaps; each back at a-full a-100 in 2.8 s (2 reads).
  speed auto 10  -> a-full a-10 in 2.8 s;  3 flaps; each back at a-full a-10 in 2.8 s.
  no speed -> a-full a-1000 in 4.5 s. LLDP neighbour 0000.cd40.0394 port1.0.2 present again at 13:54:0x (46421-u1b2.out).
  DUT log, port1.0.1 lines (readout 13:53:34 vs 13:51:5x): +12 "Port down notification received", +12 "Port up notification received",
  +9 "user shutdown", +9 "user no shutdown"  (12 = 9 user flaps + 3 renegotiations from the speed changes).
Remote flaps on 4050 port1.0.2 (steps B1/B2/B3): at a-1000 (13:54:16-13:54:35), at a-100 (13:55:xx), at a-10 (13:57:xx): 3 flaps each;
  the 4050 port read "disabled" at the first read and "connected 10 a-full a-<speed>" in 2.8 s (2 reads) after every no shutdown.
  4050 log port1.0.2 lines: 13/13 before -> 16/16 after B1 (+3/+3, 3 user shutdown) -> 19/19 after B2 (+3/+3) -> 23/23 after B3 (+4/+4:
  3 flaps + the DUT's `speed auto 10` renegotiation). 4050 LLDP table lists 00c2.8f3f.0f5d port1.0.1 after each set.
Final (13:58:xx): DUT port1.0.1 "connected trunk a-full a-1000 1000BASE-T", LLDP neighbour 0000.cd40.0394 port1.0.2 present;
  DUT log total today: 24 down / 24 up / 9 user shutdown / 9 user no shutdown (24 = 18 user flaps + 6 speed renegotiations).
  No "% " line from either unit in any call.
Side observations (not faults, recorded): the DUT port carries `switchport atmf-link` and the 4050 is not an AMF node, so the DUT logged
  `ATMF[736]: Sec_Audit - Possible DOS attack, link resets on port1.0.1 exceed limit by 4` at 00:53:36 UTC (after the 9 local flaps in
  77 s) and `Interface port1.0.1 link partner has no valid ATMF response.` at 00:53:56, 00:55:06, 00:57:48 UTC. AMF's link-reset audit
  is doing what it should on a deliberately flapped link; worth knowing when a flap test is run on an atmf-link port.

